      "section_id": "bone_lever_section_1",
      "section_title": "Bone lever",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Overview",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/bone_lever_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A bone lever is a surgical instrument used to apply controlled leverage to bone or to retract and elevate tissues around bone, depending on its design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The term can refer to different instruments. In orthopaedic surgery, a bone lever may be used to assist with bone manipulation, elevation, or reduction. Some instruments commonly described as bone levers are actually bone retractors, such as the Hohmann retractor, which holds soft tissues away from the operative field by resting against bone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE identification statement:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a bone lever, an instrument used to apply controlled leverage during bone manipulation or to assist in elevating and retracting tissues around bone, depending on its design. Its specific function is determined by the shape of the working end and the intended surgical application.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important OSCE distinction: If the displayed instrument has a broad blade or hooked end designed to hold soft tissues away from bone, identify it more specifically as a bone retractor if appropriate. Not every instrument called a bone lever is designed for the same purpose."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Labelling and identification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/bone_lever_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The exact labels depend on the particular instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Label",
                    "Component",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "A",
                      "Handle",
                      "Provides grip and control"
                    ],
                    [
                      "B",
                      "Shaft",
                      "Transfers applied force to the working end"
                    ],
                    [
                      "C",
                      "Working blade or tip",
                      "Engages bone or supports tissue retraction"
                    ],
                    [
                      "D",
                      "Curved or angled end, if present",
                      "Facilitates access around a bone surface"
                    ],
                    [
                      "E",
                      "Hook or lip, if present",
                      "Helps engage or retain tissue or bone, depending on design"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Some bone levers have a straight shaft, while others have a curved or angled end. Do not label a hook or lip unless it is visible on the actual instrument."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Properties and construction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Typical features include:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Material: Usually surgical-grade stainless steel.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Handle: Designed for a secure grip and controlled application of force.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Shaft: Straight, curved, or angled depending on the intended operative site.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Working end: May be broad, narrow, flat, curved, or hooked.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Strength: Designed to withstand the forces expected for its intended function.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Surface: Usually smooth to facilitate cleaning and reduce tissue snagging.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reusability: Many models are reusable and require validated cleaning and sterilization.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The shape and strength of the working end determine whether the instrument is suitable for manipulating bone, retracting soft tissue, or both."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/bone_lever_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Hohmann retractor"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A commonly used bone-associated retractor with a long shaft and a shaped blade or tip. It is positioned against bone to retract soft tissues during orthopaedic procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/bone_lever_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Curved bone lever"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A curved working end can help access or engage bone in anatomically confined areas, depending on the design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/bone_lever_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Bone hook or reduction lever"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Designed to engage bone or a bony fragment to assist controlled manipulation or reduction in selected procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/bone_lever_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. Bone elevator"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Used to elevate tissue from bone or assist with selected bone manipulation. Its function overlaps with some instruments casually described as bone levers, but the exact instrument name should be based on its design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam tip: Hohmann retractors, bone hooks, bone elevators, and instruments called bone levers are related in some applications, but they are not interchangeable names for every instrument. Identify the actual instrument shown."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The bone lever operates through the mechanical principle of leverage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A lever allows an applied force to produce movement or support a load around a pivot or point of contact."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "In a bone manipulation application:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "The instrument is positioned at the intended contact point.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A controlled force is applied through the handle.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The working end transmits the force to the bone or selected bony fragment.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The bone is moved or elevated as required.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In a retraction application, the instrument instead maintains displacement of soft tissues away from the bone and operative field."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key safety principle: The force must be controlled because excessive leverage may damage bone, periosteum, soft tissues, or adjacent neurovascular structures."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Uses and indications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Depending on its design, a bone lever may be used to:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Assist in manipulation or elevation of bone.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Help position selected bone fragments during reduction.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Provide leverage during selected orthopaedic procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Retract soft tissues away from a bone surface.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Improve exposure of the operative field.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assist with access to the bone during fixation or reconstruction.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_8",
              "group_title": "Surgeries in which it may be used",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Specialty",
                    "Procedure",
                    "Purpose"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Orthopaedics",
                      "Fracture reduction",
                      "Assist in controlled manipulation of selected bone fragments"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Orthopaedics",
                      "Joint replacement",
                      "Bone-associated retraction or manipulation, depending on the instrument"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Orthopaedics",
                      "Bone reconstruction",
                      "Assist exposure or positioning of bone"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Maxillofacial surgery",
                      "Selected bone procedures",
                      "Retract tissue or manipulate bone where the instrument is appropriate"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Trauma surgery",
                      "Open fracture fixation",
                      "Improve exposure or assist reduction"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Spinal surgery",
                      "Selected exposure procedures",
                      "Retract tissues using appropriate bone-associated retractors"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Not every type of bone lever is used in all these procedures; the specific design and operative technique determine its suitability."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Mechanical advantage: Helps apply force to a specific point.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Improved access: Curved or angled designs can reach selected anatomical areas.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Controlled manipulation: May assist in positioning or elevating bone.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Improved exposure: Bone-associated retractors help keep soft tissues away from the operative field.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Versatility: Available in different shapes and sizes for different surgical sites.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Durability: Suitable reusable models can withstand repeated clinical use when properly maintained.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Excessive leverage can injure bone and surrounding structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Poor positioning may cause the instrument to slip.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Some designs require substantial space to manoeuvre.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A broad blade may obscure part of the operative field.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A hooked tip may injure tissue if introduced or removed carelessly.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The instrument is not suitable for every bone manipulation or retraction task.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Incorrect identification can lead to inappropriate use.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Complications and risks",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Explanation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Bone fracture",
                      "Excessive leverage may cause an unintended fracture"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bone fragment displacement",
                      "Poorly controlled manipulation may displace a fragment"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Soft-tissue injury",
                      "The tip or blade may injure adjacent tissue"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Neurovascular injury",
                      "Particularly important when working near major nerves and vessels"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Periosteal damage",
                      "Excessive pressure or stripping can damage the periosteum"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Instrument slippage",
                      "Poor positioning may cause sudden loss of control"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Pressure injury",
                      "Prolonged or excessive retraction may damage tissues"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Instrument damage",
                      "Excessive force or misuse may deform the working end"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_12",
              "group_title": "Prevention",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Confirm the exact instrument and its intended function.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inspect the working end and handle before use.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Position the instrument under appropriate visualization.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Apply controlled force without unnecessary leverage.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Protect adjacent nerves, vessels, and soft tissues.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid prolonged, excessive pressure when using a retractor.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check the operative field for unintended tissue or bone injury.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Never use an instrument as a lever beyond its intended strength or design.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid using delicate retractors to manipulate bone when they are not designed for that purpose.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Take care when operating near nerves, vessels, and other vulnerable structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use the appropriate instrument for the specific anatomical site.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid excessive pressure against fragile bone.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ensure that the instrument is stable before applying force.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Care, cleaning and sterilization",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "For reusable instruments:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Remove gross contamination promptly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Clean the handle, shaft, working end, and any recesses thoroughly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use cleaning products compatible with the manufacturer's instructions.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Rinse and dry the instrument.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inspect the shaft for bending and the tip for deformation, cracks, or damage.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Verify that the instrument remains structurally sound.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sterilize using a validated method compatible with the instrument.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Remove bent, cracked, or damaged instruments from clinical service until appropriately assessed."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "bone_lever_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "bone_lever_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is a bone lever?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A surgical instrument used for controlled leverage during bone manipulation or, depending on its design, for retracting tissues around bone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is its mechanical principle?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Leverage: an applied force produces movement or support around a point of contact or pivot."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is a Hohmann retractor used for?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It retracts soft tissues away from bone to improve exposure during orthopaedic surgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Is a bone lever the same as a periosteal elevator?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. A periosteal elevator primarily separates periosteum from bone, whereas a bone lever is intended for leverage or manipulation, or for bone-associated retraction depending on the design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Is a bone lever the same as a bone rongeur?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. A rongeur bites and removes small pieces of bone. A bone lever applies leverage or assists manipulation or retraction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Mention four complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Unintended fracture, soft-tissue injury, neurovascular injury, and instrument slippage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Why must excessive leverage be avoided?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It can damage bone, displace fragments, injure adjacent structures, or cause the instrument to slip."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. What should be checked before use?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The integrity of the handle, shaft, and working end, as well as any bending, cracks, or deformation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. What is the difference between a bone hook and a bone retractor?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A bone hook is designed to engage bone or a fragment for manipulation, while a bone retractor is designed primarily to hold soft tissues away from the operative field."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q10. What is the main safety principle?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Apply controlled force using the correct instrument while protecting adjacent tissues and maintaining visualization."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
